the ocean currents that help to keep  britain's climate so mild  start 9 000 miles away in the chile seas  of antarctica  it's here that cold water sinks to the  deep and begins a long journey north  across the equator  warming along the way and helping to  even out temperatures around the planet  i joined scientists from british  antarctic survey as they monitored the  effect of melting ice  fresh water that then sits on the  surface of the ocean  slowing the downward plunge of the  currents there's a pattern of  circulation in the atlantic  where water comes in at one level and  goes out at a different level that's  called the atlantic overturning  circulation  of which the gulf stream is a major  component and it keeps western europe  warm  relatively benign climate all these  circulation patterns are interconnected  and the southern ocean around antarctic  is a key part of them  what the models we have and the  observations we have seem to indicate is  that  that circulation in the north atlantic  is slowing down it's all connected  the earth is one system and to  understand any part of it you have to  understand all of the parts  new research has zoomed in on the north  atlantic circulation  which transports heat from the tropics  to europe  already the system of currents called  amok have slowed by 15  and now at their weakest in more than a  millennium  but melting ice at both poles could slow  it by 34 to 45  by the end of the century changing our  climate  we may get more winter storms reaching  britain  we also expect that we may get more  summer heat waves in effect what you're  doing is you're  altering the maritime influence um  it'll also have impacts on um places  elsewhere other than the uk  so the u.s east coast may see colder  winters  it'll be impacted by sea level change  [Music]  in antarctica an iceberg the size of  bedfordshire has broken away from the  brunt ice shelf  it's a natural process not climate  change  but global warming is accelerating the  melt of polar ice  and that's having consequences in every  part of the planet  thomas more sky news
 

